    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2024534429192884475",
      "url": "https://x.com/CataPaul2/status/2024534429192884475",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/CataPaul2/status/2024534429192884475",
      "text": "Global eclipse superstitions you didn’t know about\n\n1.🇮🇳 India (IN) – Considered an inauspicious time; fasting is common, indoor stay is advised for pregnant women, and ritual cleansing follows the eclipse.\n\n2.🇨🇳 China (CN) – A dragon is believed to devour the Sun; loud noises are made to scare it away.\n\n3.🇯🇵 Japan (JP) – Once feared as bad luck; now celebrated as a scientific event.\n\n4.🇲🇽 Aztec Civilization (MX) – Seen as angry gods attacking the Sun; protective rituals were performed.\n\n5.🇳🇴 Norse Mythology (NO) – Wolves chase and try to swallow the Sun and Moon.\n\n6.🇹🇭 Thailand (TH) – Shifted from an ominous sign to a public astronomy celebration.\n\n7.🇹🇷 Turkey (TR) – Prayers and noise-making rituals are performed to restore the Sun.\n\n8.🇵🇪 Inca Civilization (PE) – The Sun god Inti is angered; ceremonies are held to calm him.\n\n9.🇰🇷 Korea (KR) – “Fire dogs” attempt to steal the Sun or Moon.\n\n10.🇻🇳 Vietnam (VN) – A giant frog or toad swallows the Sun during an eclipse.\n\n11.🇺🇸 Native American Tribes (US) – A sacred time for reflection, prayer, and renewal.\n\n12.🇫🇷 Medieval Europe (FR) – Viewed as a warning sign of war, plague, or disaster.\n\n13.🇪🇬 Ancient Egypt (EG) – A serpent attacks the Sun god Ra.\n\n14.🇬🇭 Ghana (GH) – A moment for peace-making and resolving conflicts.\n\n15.🇵🇭 Philippines (PH) – A mythical creature swallows the Moon; noise drives it away.\n\n16.🌍 Modern Global Culture – Celebrated worldwide with viewing parties and scientific study.",
      "source": "Twitter for iPhone",
